# Rust Backends & Editor Crates

## `backends/` — CLI tool binaries (called from Python)

| Package | Binary | Purpose |
|:---|:---|:---|
| `backends/ai-runtime` | `claynext-ai` | Speech quota, dub/translate prompts, VAD, OCR filter, `scan-captions` (burned-in zone sample + stitch), LLM key pool, expression tags, align-speed, ducked-volume |
| `backends/ffmpeg` | `claynext-ffmpeg` | Probe, stills, `extract-crop` (accurate capture-zone still), decode-audio → `.f32`, demux-audio WAV, export/transcode, mux |
| `backends/cpu` | `claynext-cpu` | Waveform downsample, SIMD audio stats, benchmark |
| `backends/hardware-codecs` | `claynext-codecs` | GPU detect, optimal ffmpeg encoder args |
| `backends/wgpu` | `claynext-wgpu` | GPU/WGPU device info |

- Prefer **JSON stdout** for structured results; plain text OK for prompts.
- Use UTF-8; Python bridge runs with `encoding="utf-8"`.
- Share pure functions via the crate lib (`claynext_ai_runtime::…`) and call them from the bin.
- Workspace build: `cargo build -p claynext-ai-runtime --bin claynext-ai` (set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` to repo `target/` when needed).

## `crates/editor-*` — shared editor domain

Notable crates: `editor-dubbing` (plan, alignment, pipeline), `editor-speech`, `editor-types`, `editor-timeline`, `editor-media`, `editor-ai`, …

- Keep **pure timing/plan logic** here or in `ai-runtime`; avoid duplicating formulas in Python.
- `editor-dubbing` should reuse `claynext_ai_runtime` helpers (e.g. `align_speech_speed`) when possible.
- Serialize plan fields (`translate_fit_duration`, `translate_slang`, `translate_expression`) with serde defaults for backward compatibility.

## Do not

- Reimplement the same algorithm only in Python when a Rust function already exists.
- Add heavy GUI dependencies to Rust crates.
- Break the Python adapter contract without updating `src/claynext/adapters/`.
